# The cap ratchet's baseline. Rule 4.1, `plan/05:352-354`:
#
#   "the architecture test FAILS ON A CAP INCREASE IN THE DIFF, not just on a
#    violation. Vellum's caps only ever went down."
#
# That test did not exist. The default cap went 400 -> 800 with a green suite,
# because nothing was checking -- the rule was a wish (`plan/05` §0) for the
# whole of Milestone 1 and its tail. This file is the mechanism.
#
# HOW IT WORKS. `the_cap_ratchet_only_turns_down` in tests/ratchet.rs reads this
# file and compares it with the live constants in tests/file_rules.rs. A cap
# that is LOWER than its line here passes and is a ratchet turn. A cap that is
# HIGHER fails, and the only way to make it pass is to edit this file in the same
# commit -- which puts the increase in the diff, in a file that exists for no
# other purpose, where a reviewer cannot miss it.
#
# That is the honest version of "fails on a cap increase in the diff": a test
# cannot read a diff, but it can make an increase impossible to make quietly.
#
# WHEN YOU DO RAISE ONE. Say why here, in the commit, with a measurement. The
# 400 -> 800 change is the model: it was the author's call, it cited VellumFE's
# own caps (3600/2100/1700/1100/800/700) as evidence that 400 was invented, and
# it counted the cost -- five splits in one milestone.
#
# FORMAT: `<name> <value>`, one per line. `#` comments and blank lines ignored.

# The cap every file gets unless CAP_EXCEPTIONS names it.
#
# Was 400, raised to 800 by the author on 2026-09-18 after 400 forced five
# splits in one milestone. 400 was invented with no Cena code to calibrate
# against; 800 is below all but one of VellumFE's shipped caps.
DEFAULT_MAX_LINES 800

# How many named exceptions may exist at all.
#
# ONE: crates/cena-model/data/crit_tables.tsv, at 2400. It is the only file in
# the workspace for which "move code down" has no meaning -- it is DATA, 2,394
# table entries with no functions to move anywhere.
#
# A table that GROWS is a cap that is failing: every entry is a file that was
# allowed to keep growing instead of being split. This file's own 650-line
# exception was removed when the default rose to 800, which is the direction the
# count should travel.
#
# Recorded because I guessed 0 here and the test caught it -- which is the guard
# working on its author before anyone else.
#
# RAISED 1 -> 4 on 2026-09-20, for the creature port. The three new entries are
# `creature_messages.tsv` (3,862 rows), `creature_attacks.tsv` (1,603) and
# `creature_areas.tsv` (1,394) -- the bestiary ported from Lich's 627 template
# files per plan/13 section 4a, generated by
# `crates/cena-model/tools/extract_creatures.rb`.
#
# THE TEST IS RIGHT THAT THIS IS THE WRONG DIRECTION, so here is the honest
# accounting rather than a claim that these are special.
#
# What makes them the same case as crit_tables.tsv, not a new one: they are
# DATA, one fact per row, with no functions to move anywhere, and no seam to
# split on -- a split would be an arbitrary alphabetical cut through one sorted
# sequence. plan/13:125 specifies this exact shape for this exact kind of row.
#
# What makes them THREE entries rather than one: they are three different
# cardinalities over the same 627 creatures -- a creature has many areas, many
# attacks and many messages. One wide table would need 1,394 area columns.
# MEASURED: the fourth table, `creatures.tsv`, is 628 lines and takes NO
# exception, which is the cap still binding the part of this port that fits.
#
# The caps are 4200 / 1800 / 1600 against 3,863 / 1,604 / 1,395 actual lines --
# roughly 10% headroom, so a regeneration that grew the bestiary materially
# still goes red and gets looked at, the same reasoning crit_tables.tsv's 2400
# records for its 2,394 rows.
#
# The direction to travel is still down. If the bestiary is ever split by
# hunting area or loaded from one file, these should collapse back.
#
# RAISED 4 -> 5 on 2026-09-20, for `menu_commands.tsv` at 1300 (1,106 actual
# rows). Same case as the four above and no new argument: DATA, one row per
# line, a flat sequence keyed by coordinate with no seam to split on, and
# plan/13:125 specifies this exact shape.
#
# What it buys: a context menu arrives as bare coordinates and NOTHING else --
# measured over 425 menu items in the corpus, zero carry a label or a command.
# Without this table the menu is a list of numbers, and the wire offers no
# fallback.
#
# THE HONEST COST, because this note exists to record it rather than to argue
# the entry is special: the table grew by one and the default cap is what
# should have absorbed this instead. It did not, because the file is 1,106
# lines and the default is 800.
#
# What was NOT done to make room: `tests/file_rules.rs` went to 804 of 800
# when this entry was added, and it was SPLIT rather than exempted -- Rule 2.1
# and its allowlist moved to `tests/raw_text_escapes.rs`. That file has now
# gone red five times and split five times without once raising its own cap,
# which is the behaviour this whole mechanism is for.
#
# The 1300 is ~18% headroom over 1,106. It is deliberately tight because this
# file is KNOWN to grow: regenerating it from the live client's copy rather
# than the 2003 one the reference ships added 514 rows in a single step, with
# nothing removed. The next regeneration should go red and be looked at.
#
# RAISED 5 -> 6 on 2026-09-24, for `creature_lists.tsv` at 3650 (3,309
# lines), and the creature caps raised with it: messages 4200 -> 7800 (7,126
# lines) and attacks 1800 -> 3500 (3,175 lines).
#
# The cause is a decision, not growth: the author, 2026-09-24, *"The reason it
# was all there was multiple reasons, one of which is a comprehensive
# beastiary, the other is the messages have uses just because they haven't
# been made apparent yet."* The first port kept 4 of 13 message kinds, one of
# six attack categories and none of the plain lists. Porting the rest roughly
# doubled two tables and added a third.
#
# Same case as the entries above and no new argument: DATA, one row per line,
# no seam to split on -- splitting messages by kind would put attack lines and
# flee lines of one creature in two files for no reader's benefit. Headroom is
# ~10% on each, as before, so the next regeneration that grows the bestiary
# materially still goes red. The three small new tables (creature_info 51,
# creature_abilities 80) and creatures.tsv (628) take no exception.
MAX_CAP_EXCEPTIONS 6

# EACH EXCEPTION'S OWN CAP. Added 2026-09-23 (review finding 3).
#
# The two numbers above guarded the default and the COUNT of exceptions, and
# nothing guarded the caps inside them: raising crit_tables.tsv from 2400 to
# 24000 changed neither number and passed green. An exception table with a
# fixed count and raisable entries is five caps with no ratchet.
#
# So each exception's cap is recorded here too, as `exception:<path> <cap>`,
# at the value it carries today in tests/file_rules.rs. The reasoning for each
# is already written beside the entry there (and above, for the four that
# raised MAX_CAP_EXCEPTIONS); these lines are the ratchet, not a restatement.
# `the_cap_ratchet_only_turns_down` fails on a raise, on an exception with no
# line here, and on a line here with no exception.
exception:crates/cena-model/data/creature_messages.tsv 7800
exception:crates/cena-model/data/creature_attacks.tsv 3500
exception:crates/cena-model/data/creature_lists.tsv 3650
exception:crates/cena-model/data/creature_areas.tsv 1600
exception:crates/cena-model/data/menu_commands.tsv 1300
exception:crates/cena-model/data/crit_tables.tsv 2400

# SPLIT PARENTS -- Rule 4.4's other half. `plan/05:385-388` cites Vellum's
# `split_parents_stay_facades` BY NAME, and Cena implemented only the
# `lib.rs`/`mod.rs` half: nine files with child modules -- state.rs, actor.rs,
# supervisor.rs, parser.rs, frame.rs and four more -- were governed by the 800
# default and by nothing else.
#
# Vellum's version is not a no-behavior rule. A split parent legitimately holds
# type definitions and dispatchers; `GameState::apply` belongs in `state.rs`.
# It is a PER-FILE CAP that only ratchets down, and its comment is the whole
# intent: "if one trips, move code down into a submodule instead of raising the
# cap."
#
# RE-BASELINED 2026-09-19, by the author, after the first version failed at the
# one thing this comment claims.
#
# The rule was "the measured size rounded up to the next 50". MEASURED against
# what that produced: all nine files sat within 44 lines of their cap, and
# `state.rs` within ONE.
#
#   probe.rs 283/300  crit.rs 227/250  state.rs 499/500  frame.rs 445/450
#   parser.rs 424/450  tags.rs 306/350  text.rs 376/400  actor.rs 586/600
#   supervisor.rs 557/600
#
# That is not "enough that an ordinary edit does not trip it". Rounding a 462-line
# file up to 500 gives 38 lines, and ordinary M2 work then spent 37 of them --
# so the cap stopped meaning "this file should not exceed 500" and came to mean
# "this file was 462 once". Correcting a stale doc comment in `state.rs` had to
# be written NET-NEUTRAL to fit, and a milestone's first step was shaped by
# arithmetic rather than by cohesion.
#
# THE RULE IS NOW measured size + 50, rounded up to the next 50, which makes the
# headroom a floor rather than a remainder. Applied to all nine at once, giving
# 51-94 lines each. Every one is still far below DEFAULT_MAX_LINES.
#
# WHAT DID NOT CHANGE: the default is 800, set by the author on 2026-09-18, and
# these nine are a SECOND and stricter rule (4.4) layered under it. Two numbers
# governing one file is confusing enough that the author asked where 500 came
# from -- the answer being that nobody chose it. If a cap here ever needs to
# rise again, say why, with a measurement, as this note does.
crates/cena-model/src/crit.rs 300
crates/cena-model/src/state.rs 550
crates/cena-protocol/src/frame.rs 500
crates/cena-protocol/src/parser.rs 500
crates/cena-protocol/src/tags.rs 400
crates/cena-protocol/src/text.rs 450
crates/cena-session/src/actor.rs 650
crates/cena-session/src/supervisor.rs 650

# DISCOVERED, NOT LISTED -- fourteen more, added 2026-09-23 (review finding 4).
#
# The nine above were written in by hand on 2026-09-19 and nothing found the
# next one. `split_parents_stay_facades` now discovers every `name.rs` with a
# sibling `name/` of `.rs` files and fails on one with no line here. That found
# fourteen, among them the three largest split parents in the workspace.
#
# Each is capped by the rule above -- measured size + 50, rounded up to the
# next 50 -- measured at HEAD 2ad1209, and CLAMPED TO DEFAULT_MAX_LINES: a
# split-parent cap above 800 would be a number the default already overrides.
#
#   travel.rs 661  drive.rs 750  routines.rs 297  day_pass.rs 612
#   binary.rs 101  character.rs 797  combat.rs 123  combat/parse.rs 239
#   creature.rs 540  creatures.rs 535  societies.rs 280  gemstone.rs 62
#   weblogin.rs 85  combat_recorder.rs 449
#
# THE HONEST COST: these are recordings of what exists, not judgements that it
# is right. Three sit at or near the default and are split parents in name
# only -- state/character.rs has 3 lines of headroom, travel/drive.rs 50,
# travel.rs 89 -- which is the shape Rule 4.4 exists to prevent: the parent
# kept growing after the split. They are baselined rather than failed because
# the rule that would have caught them did not exist; they can only ratchet
# down from here, and the author should decide whether to tighten them.
crates/cena-behavior/src/travel.rs 750
crates/cena-behavior/src/travel/drive.rs 800
crates/cena-behavior/src/travel/routines.rs 350
crates/cena-behavior/src/travel/routines/day_pass.rs 700
crates/cena-map/src/binary.rs 200
crates/cena-model/src/state/character.rs 800
crates/cena-model/src/state/combat.rs 200
crates/cena-model/src/state/combat/parse.rs 300
crates/cena-model/src/state/creature.rs 600
crates/cena-model/src/state/creatures.rs 600
crates/cena-model/src/state/societies.rs 350
crates/cena-platform/src/gemstone.rs 150
crates/cena-platform/src/gemstone/weblogin.rs 150
crates/cena-session/src/combat_recorder.rs 500

